WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:03.610
The star that you saw in your last video was
in fact not a star, it was Jupiter.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:07.129
You can tell that it's a planet one, because
it is so bright and two, because planets do

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:08.820
not twinkle the way that stars do.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.460
This is because planets are much closer to
us, so instead of seeing just a point of light

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.680
like we do with stars, we actually see lots
of points of light in a tiny, tiny little

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:16.680
disk.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.350
The moving different layers of different densities
of our atmosphere cause the light from the

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:24.071
single point source to jiggle, and the light
from the little disks jiggle as well, but

00:00:24.071 --> 00:00:28.119
all the different points tend to average each
other out, so planets appear much more constant

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:29.119
in their brightness.

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:31.610
So twinkle, twinkle little star- stars don't
actually twinkle.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:35.469
They are also very constant in their brightness;
our atmosphere just makes it look like they're

00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:36.469
twinkling.

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:38.079
Another lie we tell to children!

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:39.829
Like the tortoise and the hare.

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:40.829
That's just- no.

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:44.350
I don't care how much the hare messes around,
the hare's gonna win.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.769
A hare's top speed is like 45 miles per hour.

00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:48.260
Slow and steady doesn't win the race, John.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.800
You know who wins the race?

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.899
Fast and steady, almost exclusively.

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:56.199
Though, it occurs to me, having said this,
that hares actually have very short lifespans

00:00:56.199 --> 00:00:58.030
and tortoises live for, like, ever.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:03.350
So maybe if the "race" is the amount of distance
an animal can cover in its lifetime, maybe

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:04.890
then, maybe a tortoise could win?

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.119
I'm gonna do some math.

00:01:06.119 --> 00:01:07.119
Okay, we're back!

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:11.270
No, um, the oldest tortoise in the world is
named Jonathan, and he is 182 years old.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:15.609
His top speed is 0.17 miles per hour, meaning
that if he travelled every hour of his life,

00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:19.710
and there have been more than 1.5 million
of them, he would have by now travelled about

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.710
271 thousand miles.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:25.600
At 45 miles per hour, a hare would only need
13% of its five year life span to eclipse

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.600
the tortoise.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.321
So yeah, hare versus Jonathan, in a really
terribly hypothetical in which neither of

00:01:31.321 --> 00:01:33.780
them ever sleep or rest, the hare still wins.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:37.240
Though the hare has been dead for a hundred
and seventy seven years...

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.240
So.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.729
Maybe the real moral of the story is Slow
and Steady Loses the Race But Who Cares As

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:44.729
Long As You're Not Dead.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:48.500
Thanks to Jonathan the oldest tortoise, and
also the oldest John, for teaching us that

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.500
one.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.760
Come on, John, you tell the history of Johns
and you forget the oldest John ever?

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.960
You're slacking, that's all I'm saying.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.429
Here's another interesting bit of John trivia:
there might be more forms of John than there

00:01:57.429 --> 00:01:59.770
are forms of any other name.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:05.299
Jane, Gianni, Juan, Ivan, Janice, Joan, Ian,
Yanni-

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:06.299
Yeah, Yanni.

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:07.299
These are just a few.

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:08.720
Lots of them start with that J noise, 'J-uh'.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.303
But if you say a J noise without messing with
the air as it's coming out of your mouth,

00:02:12.303 --> 00:02:13.303
it's just a Y noise.

00:02:13.303 --> 00:02:15.000
You have John, and you have Yahn.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.660
And Y noises are weird, because they are never
consonants.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.360
Yet another lie that we tell to children!

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.030
Y noises are two vowel sounds shoved together
really quick.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.470
Like with 'your', you don't get to a consonant
until you get to the r.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:28.470
It's just 'Ee-yore'.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.470
Eeyore.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.350
Eeyore, Eeyore, your, your, your, your.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.920
W's are like this too.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.080
When you say Wednesday, you- there's no consonant.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.080
You say 'Oo-ens-day'.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.080
It's just vowels!

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.520
When two vowel sounds are slapped together,
you get one of the coolest words in the English

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.160
language, the 'dipthong'.'Dipthongs' are everywhere,
we're pretty bad at hearing them, but even

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.510
in simple words, like Mike- 'mi-eek'. 'a-ee'.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.920
You see it written down sometimes, like 'oil',
o-i-l.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.230
When we put a 'dipthong' in place of a consonant,
as breaking two syllables apart, it basically

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:56.120
is acting like a consonant, but it's not a
consonant.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.680
So we call it semi-vowel.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.090
In fact, the name that 'John' came from, 'Iohannes,'
it's actually all spelled out there for everybody

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:03.420
to see, all the vowels.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.960
It also shows the origin of the silent H,
because John basically used to be Johan.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.860
Now for a piece of seemingly random information:
Middle English used the suffix '-kin' to put

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.570
on the end of words that meant it was a small
thing.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.380
Like if there was a kid named Tom, you would
call him Tomkin.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.030
Or a small piece of cloth, would be called
a clothkin, except their word for cloth was

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.280
nap, so it would be a napkin.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:26.900
The Middle English shortened Johan to Han,
and when there was a little person named Han,

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.130
they would call him Hankin.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:32.790
Which, I am not even kidding, is how the name
Hank first appeared.

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.620
John, you and I have the same name!

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:49.069
I'll see you on Monday.

